# KIRA — ISR / C-UAS Unmanned Aerial System

**Preliminary design and system architecture of a long-endurance fixed-wing ISR / Counter-UAS platform**

**Institution:** Politecnico di Milano  
**Role:** Team Leader, 5-member group  
**Academic Year:** 2024–2025  
**Project Type:** Aircraft Design / Preliminary UAS Design

---

## Project Overview

KIRA is a fixed-wing Unmanned Aerial System developed as a preliminary-design study for persistent **Intelligence, Surveillance and Reconnaissance (ISR)** missions with **Counter-UAS capability**.

The platform was designed around:

- long-endurance persistent surveillance;
- detection, classification and tracking of aerial threats;
- modular mission payload integration;
- mobile ground-control operations;
- rapid deployment from semi-prepared sites;
- single- and dual-UAV operating concepts.

The design targeted a system capable of combining aircraft-level performance with a deployable mission architecture rather than treating the air vehicle as an isolated platform.

---

## My Role

I acted as **Team Leader of a five-member engineering team**, coordinating the one-year conceptual and preliminary design activity.

My work included:

- project and technical coordination;
- definition of the operational scenario and CONOPS;
- aircraft configuration trade-offs;
- mission-driven preliminary sizing;
- system architecture definition;
- payload and mission-system integration;
- performance and endurance assessment;
- aircraft-level mass, power and CG integration;
- preliminary structural sizing;
- ground-control-system concept definition;
- integration of multidisciplinary design outputs into a coherent UAS concept.

---

## Mission and CONOPS

KIRA was designed for persistent ISR and C-UAS operations in environments where fixed ground-based systems may be limited by terrain, infrastructure or mobility.

The operating concept considered:

### Single-UAV Operations

A single aircraft performs persistent surveillance around the protected area from a defined loiter orbit.

### Dual-UAV Operations

Two aircraft operate on coordinated opposing orbits to increase persistent coverage and support continuous surveillance of the target area.

The concept also included expeditionary deployment from **semi-prepared or grass runways** with limited infrastructure.

---

## System Architecture

The complete UAS concept was structured around three main elements:

### Air Vehicle

High-aspect-ratio fixed-wing configuration optimised primarily for endurance and persistent surveillance.

### Mission Payload

A modular mission architecture including:

- **GMTI / SAR radar**
- **EO/IR sensors**
- **Electronic Warfare / EW payloads**

The mission bay was designed to support payload modularity and future configuration changes.

### Ground Control Station

A **mobile GCS** concept was developed to support both single- and dual-UAV operations.

The complete deployment concept used a two-vehicle logistics architecture:

- one vehicle for transport of the UAV platforms;
- one vehicle containing the mobile GCS, operators, communication systems and mission-support equipment.

---

## Key Design Targets

| Parameter | Design Value |
|---|---:|
| Maximum Take-Off Mass | **640 kg** |
| Mission Payload | **125 kg** |
| Maximum Endurance | **25 h** |
| Wing Span | **10 m** |
| Maximum Speed | **115 m/s** |
| Take-off Distance | **< 400 m** |
| Landing Distance | **< 400 m** |
| Payload Electrical Budget | **~3 kW** |
| Available APU Electrical Power | **11 kW** |

The baseline ISR configuration was sized to achieve approximately **25 hours of endurance with the full 125 kg mission payload**.

---

## Mission-System Integration

The design required aircraft-level integration of several high-level mission systems rather than independent sizing of each subsystem.

The system architecture considered:

- radar integration;
- EO/IR payloads;
- EW equipment;
- onboard electrical power requirements;
- avionics;
- flight-control actuators;
- communication architecture;
- mobile GCS operations;
- mission payload modularity;
- dual-UAV coordination.

A dedicated electrical-power budget was developed to ensure that payload and avionics requirements remained compatible with available onboard power.

---

## Aircraft-Level Design

The preliminary design process included:

- mission requirement definition;
- configuration selection;
- aerodynamic sizing;
- endurance-driven wing design;
- propulsion sizing;
- aircraft mass breakdown;
- payload integration;
- centre-of-gravity management;
- preliminary structural sizing;
- electrical-power budgeting;
- operational performance assessment.

The selected configuration used a **high-aspect-ratio wing** to support long loiter endurance while maintaining suitable low-speed handling and field-operability characteristics.

---

## Stability and CG Management

A dedicated fuel-management strategy was considered to control centre-of-gravity movement throughout the long-duration mission.

The preliminary design maintained the CG inside the prescribed stability envelope during the full mission profile.

---

## Structures and Airworthiness

Preliminary structural sizing was carried out using **STANAG 4671** as the provisional design basis.

The design process included:

- manoeuvre and gust load envelopes;
- aerodynamic load estimation;
- composite structural sizing;
- wing-root shear and bending assessment;
- bending–torsion evaluation;
- preliminary laminate sizing.

A dual-use compliance strategy was considered, combining a civil certification baseline with later military validation as required.

---

## Counter-UAS Architecture

The C-UAS mission concept was based primarily on:

- airborne detection and tracking;
- radar / EO-IR sensor integration;
- electronic-warfare payload capability;
- communication with the Ground Control Station;
- coordinated UAV operations;
- persistent surveillance around a protected area.

The system was conceived as an **ISR platform with integrated C-UAS mission capability**, rather than as a dedicated kinetic interceptor.

---

## Design Maturity

**Estimated maturity: TRL 2–3**

The project remained at **conceptual / preliminary design level**.

No full-scale hardware prototype or flight-test campaign was performed as part of the project.

Future maturation steps identified in the design roadmap included:

- interface definition and payload mock-ups;
- avionics and flight-control HIL testing;
- EMI/EMC ground testing;
- full-scale prototype manufacturing;
- progressive flight-envelope expansion.

